\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces What this survey actually sampled in frequency. Each searched spectral window is drawn as a horizontal segment on the row of its host system, rows ordered by distance, coloured by that window's nominal EIRP$_{5\sigma }$ threshold; the upper panel counts systems covering each gigahertz of sky frequency. The 92.4\,GHz frequency union is assembled from 34 disjoint islands of median width 1.8\,GHz, concentrated near 230 and 345\,GHz, and the summed window bandwidth exceeds the union by a factor 7.6 -- the coverage is narrow, clustered and repeatedly revisited, not broad. These are the intervals on which the occurrence limits of \S  \ref  {sec:discussion} are conditioned. Thresholds are nominal in the sense of \S  \ref  {sec:method}.}}{15}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:waterfall}{{7}{15}{What this survey actually sampled in frequency. Each searched spectral window is drawn as a horizontal segment on the row of its host system, rows ordered by distance, coloured by that window's nominal EIRP$_{5\sigma }$ threshold; the upper panel counts systems covering each gigahertz of sky frequency. The 92.4\,GHz frequency union is assembled from 34 disjoint islands of median width 1.8\,GHz, concentrated near 230 and 345\,GHz, and the summed window bandwidth exceeds the union by a factor 7.6 -- the coverage is narrow, clustered and repeatedly revisited, not broad. These are the intervals on which the occurrence limits of \S \ref {sec:discussion} are conditioned. Thresholds are nominal in the sense of \S \ref {sec:method}}{figure.7}{}}
